WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.650
Hello and welcome back to another video
with ItsDrDan and today we are going to be

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:07.340
learning about the conservation of mass.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:09.110
So what is it all about?

00:00:09.129 --> 00:00:13.910
Well, the conservation of mass is
a law, so let's quickly refer to

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:17.870
what is a theory versus law, just so
we're familiar with those concepts.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:24.030
So a theory is a proposed explanation
that is based on the examination of fact.

00:00:24.389 --> 00:00:27.490
So if you see a bunch of observations,
you're measuring a bunch of things.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:30.900
And then you want to theorize exactly
what it is that you're seeing.

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:34.559
So this is something about like
how maybe new medicines or new

00:00:34.559 --> 00:00:37.780
vaccines or how maybe we can make
rockets that can go to the moon.

00:00:38.099 --> 00:00:41.839
It's all trying to answer
those why type of questions.

00:00:42.409 --> 00:00:48.189
Now, a law is something that's based on
a natural process, meaning that it's, it

00:00:48.189 --> 00:00:51.249
behaves exact same way, no matter what.

00:00:51.585 --> 00:00:54.425
Because that is how our world works.

00:00:54.795 --> 00:00:57.645
So, for example, the law of gravity.

00:00:57.675 --> 00:01:02.015
No matter what, if I take a, an
object and I were to drop it,

00:01:02.205 --> 00:01:04.625
it's going to fall no matter what.

00:01:05.084 --> 00:01:10.755
Um, so for this one, the law of
conservation of mass is one of those.

00:01:11.215 --> 00:01:12.515
Now what is this all about?

00:01:12.585 --> 00:01:17.575
Well, it's about that mass
cannot be created nor destroyed

00:01:17.655 --> 00:01:19.194
via chemical reactions.

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:24.670
So however much reactant you have going
in or how much mass, you should get

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:27.400
it out as a different type of product.

00:01:27.809 --> 00:01:31.460
So if you measure a certain mass at
the very beginning and it combined with

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:35.129
another ingredient that you measured to,
you should get the same amount afterwards.

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:38.220
Now, bear in mind, there could be all
kinds of things that could go wrong,

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:42.600
maybe the reaction is not perfectly
complete, but it is something that's

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:47.020
been observed time and time again
for as long as humanity has existed,

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.730
it is a constant law that exists.

00:01:50.019 --> 00:01:52.389
Let's take a look at
what this is all about.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:06.590
As I said before, this is all
about how we can conserve mass.

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:11.339
Meaning that let's say if I have
a reaction and I have a bunch of

00:02:11.339 --> 00:02:16.049
little atoms trying to go in to
this chemical reaction and I want

00:02:16.049 --> 00:02:18.170
to ask what exactly will happen?

00:02:18.419 --> 00:02:20.960
What should I get out afterwards?

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:25.790
So if I have three yellow atoms and two
red ones and they were to go through

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:31.190
the chemical reaction as reactants,
according to the law of conservation

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:33.905
of mass, All of them should come out.

00:02:33.945 --> 00:02:38.924
Now, if it's a chemical change or
a chemical reaction, so where we

00:02:38.924 --> 00:02:43.834
have change occurring and we can
see that change, where everything

00:02:43.875 --> 00:02:46.294
makes a brand new product.

00:02:46.765 --> 00:02:49.534
Notice how I still have
three yellow atoms.

00:02:49.945 --> 00:02:54.734
Two red ones, but they look completely
different because a reaction took place.

00:02:55.094 --> 00:03:00.355
However, I still have the same mass
because if I add up all those individual

00:03:00.374 --> 00:03:03.234
atoms, I will still get the same number.

00:03:03.785 --> 00:03:07.325
So atoms are rearranged
during a chemical reaction.

00:03:07.424 --> 00:03:09.474
They are never lost.

00:03:09.475 --> 00:03:13.385
So they always exist as something
whether it's a product or a reactant,

00:03:13.935 --> 00:03:19.180
meaning that the mass of any element
at the beginning of a reaction is will

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:23.660
always be equal to the mass of the
elements at the end of the reaction.

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:26.660
So if I have all these little
pieces, you should be able to add

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:30.599
them up, and you should have the
same amount of mass on both sides.

00:03:31.039 --> 00:03:34.930
So the mass of the reactants always
equals the mass of the products.

00:03:35.339 --> 00:03:38.919
And so let's take a look at how
we can use this in some examples.

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:42.440
Let's say I have a combustion
reaction, meaning that we're

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:44.109
going to burn something.

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:49.149
So similar to, let's say if you have a gas
stove or a propane grill at home, you burn

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:53.439
the gas with, and it needs oxygen for it
to breathe in order for that to happen.

00:03:53.990 --> 00:03:56.700
Well, what does it make afterwards
if you were to burn something?

00:03:56.749 --> 00:04:00.100
Well, one of the things that you
might see or notice is smoke, right?

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:00.989
So CO2.

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:04.920
One of the other ingredients that
can occur as well will be made as

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:09.469
well as water, which you can see is
water vapor, um, as well, it's one of

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:11.230
the other things that gets created.

00:04:11.659 --> 00:04:16.839
So if I have a combustion reaction here, I
have methane gas and I have oxygen atoms.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:18.269
Notice how they're all together.

00:04:18.675 --> 00:04:22.465
And it's going to combust, and we're
going to be making CO2 and water.

00:04:23.094 --> 00:04:26.395
Now, let's say we were
to measure this reaction.

00:04:26.805 --> 00:04:30.814
Meaning we're going to measure the mass of
each of the different ingredients in it.

00:04:31.275 --> 00:04:36.490
So, I measured it, and I found that
there were 16 grams of methane in

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:42.630
the reaction, and I notice that
there's also 64 grams of oxygen, and

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:45.120
it combusts to make CO2 and water.

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:45.940
Okay?

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:46.500
Great.

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:48.869
So we want to highlight all those
important pieces of information

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:49.829
as we go through these, right?

00:04:49.829 --> 00:04:52.639
It's something I've talked about in
many of my videos as we go through them.

00:04:53.319 --> 00:04:55.739
Put the information together,
figure out what you're given.

00:04:56.380 --> 00:05:02.400
Now it says we also measure 44
grams of CO2, which is created.

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:07.690
Now I'm asking, or I desire,
is how much water should we

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:11.045
make after a complete reaction.

00:05:11.385 --> 00:05:14.335
So how much water should there be?

00:05:14.724 --> 00:05:15.895
Now what are we going to do?

00:05:15.895 --> 00:05:19.455
I already have a balanced chemical
reaction, which for many students

00:05:19.455 --> 00:05:22.474
at this point is something you
will learn later in the semester.

00:05:22.825 --> 00:05:26.715
So we're going to write down all the
pieces of information that we were given.

00:05:27.304 --> 00:05:34.184
So we have 16 grams of methane,
we have 64 grams of oxygen,

00:05:34.805 --> 00:05:38.145
and we have 44 grams of CO2.

00:05:39.325 --> 00:05:45.575
And we want to know how many grams
of H2O do I have in this problem.

00:05:46.175 --> 00:05:51.535
Now, from this, if you remember from what
I said before, the mass of the reactants

00:05:51.545 --> 00:05:53.755
is equal to the mass of the products.

00:05:54.055 --> 00:05:56.635
So what exactly should I do?

00:05:56.964 --> 00:05:59.765
So this is just simply
just a algebra problem.

00:06:00.185 --> 00:06:04.615
We'll add up both sides and solve
for x essentially, which is water.

00:06:05.105 --> 00:06:08.495
So if I take this and re and kind of
rewrite it for ourselves, let's do that.

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:12.830
So we have 16 grams of CO2.

00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:16.910
We have 64 grams of O2 as reactants.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:23.889
And that's equal to 44
grams of CO2 plus X.

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:26.019
So let's solve for X.

00:06:26.409 --> 00:06:29.580
So on the left hand side we'll
have a total of 80 grams.

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:34.790
We will move over the 44
grams from both sides.

00:06:35.635 --> 00:06:42.415
And what we will see as a result is
that x is going to be equal to 36 grams.

00:06:42.415 --> 00:06:46.125
So 80 minus 44 is 36.

00:06:46.654 --> 00:06:49.555
Now, does that make sense?

00:06:49.875 --> 00:06:51.495
Well, let's take a look at it.

00:06:51.985 --> 00:06:57.520
So if we know that water
was 36 grams, which is H2O.

00:06:58.309 --> 00:07:01.179
And we know that CO2 is 44 grams.

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:03.320
What is the total?

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:04.730
80 grams.

00:07:05.179 --> 00:07:08.080
And what was the thing that we
had on the left side earlier?

00:07:08.429 --> 00:07:12.049
Well if you notice, earlier
we had 80 grams as reactants.

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:16.919
So notice that the law of conservation
of mass exists and we have the

00:07:16.919 --> 00:07:18.940
same amount of mass on each sides.

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:23.345
So if you have a complete reaction,
Or even something that maybe it

00:07:23.375 --> 00:07:27.505
doesn't completely go, go through,
if you measure everything, you

00:07:27.505 --> 00:07:28.865
should still have the same amount.

00:07:29.095 --> 00:07:29.455
Okay.

00:07:30.064 --> 00:07:31.995
So this has been the conservation of mass.

00:07:32.005 --> 00:07:33.725
I hope that this video helped you.

00:07:34.115 --> 00:07:37.254
Thank you so much for listening
and please hit that like and

00:07:37.255 --> 00:07:38.655
subscribe button if you haven't.

00:07:39.044 --> 00:07:43.194
Uh, there'll be more chemistry content
to come and thank you all so much.

